  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe latest surface analysis and satellite/radar imagery show a \nprimary cold front exiting northeast Wisconsin early this morning,\nwhile a secondary cold front extends from western Lake Superior \ninto central Minnesota. Upper-level troughing is settling across \nthe Upper Midwest and Great Lakes while an amplified ridge builds \nover the Intermountain West. This pattern leaves the region under \ncool, cyclonic northwest flow aloft today into midweek. Upstream, \na broad area of stratocumulus with bases between 1500 and 3500 \nfeet exists behind the secondary front, with moderately strong \ncold air advection dropping 850mb temperatures into the lower \nteens Celsius. A high-pressure system across the central Plains \nwill gradually shift eastward toward the Mid-Mississippi Valley \nthrough Wednesday, eventually anchoring stable and comfortable \nweather over the Western Great Lakes through the end of the work \nweek.\n\nAs the secondary cold front drops southeast across northern \nWisconsin today, mid-level height falls will cool temperatures \naloft into the tenth percentile. Strong daytime heating will \nsteepen low-level lapse rates, producing diurnal instability in \nthe 300 to 600 J/kg range across northeast and east-central \nWisconsin. Scattered showers currently confined to far north-\ncentral Wisconsin will expand southward through late morning and \nafternoon. An isolated rumble of thunder cannot be ruled out \nwithin the stronger convective cores, but severe weather is not \nexpected due to weak instability. In addition, deep boundary-\nlayer mixing will tap into stronger winds aloft, generating gusty\nnorthwest winds of 20 to 30 mph across the forecast area this \nafternoon. These winds will generate hazardous waves in the\nnearshore waters, prompting Small Craft Advisories on the Bay of \nGreen Bay and nearshore waters of Lake Michigan into early \nWednesday morning. Showers will quickly wane late this afternoon \nand evening with loss of heating. Clearing skies tonight will \nclear the way for a sunny and comfortable Wednesday with high \ntemperatures in the mid 70s. \n\nModels show minimal potential for near-surface smoke or \nvisibility reductions, so smoke has been excluded from the \nforecast.\n\nFor the long-term period from Thursday through Monday, the western\nGreat Lakes will remain positioned downstream of the broad ridge \nbuilding over the Intermountain West, maintaining northwest flow \naloft through Friday. This persistent northwest flow will keep the\nmain reservoir of deep instability suppressed well south of the \nregion, ensuring seasonable temperatures and pleasant humidity \nlevels. Highs through Friday will reside in the upper 70s to low \n80s under mostly clear to partly cloudy skies. By Saturday and \nSunday, the upper-level flow pattern begins to flatten, allowing \nGulf moisture and an unstable airmass to surge northward. High \ntemperatures will climb into the upper 80s to near 90 in central \nWisconsin on Sunday, accompanied by rising dewpoints in the upper \n60s. Precipitation chances will gradually increase this weekend \n(30 to 50 percent chance) as shortwave impulses embedded within \nthe flattening flow interact with the returning instability. While\nmachine learning guidance indicates only a subtle severe weather \nthreat at this time, details on timing and mesoscale organization \nwill need to be monitored in upcoming forecast updates.",
